# DC-1 amber frontlight node setup.
#
# Gate 3 of 3 for rootless amber control: the kernel node is root:root 0644,
# so even with the TE allow + mlstrustedobject (sepolicy/dc1amber.te) the
# app's write fails EACCES on plain DAC. init opens the mode; SELinux still
# restricts writers to the domains policy allows (hal_light, init,
# platform_app, system_app).
#
# The early write also lights the frontlight out of the box: on a fresh
# /data the AmberControl package can still be in the first-boot "stopped"
# state, which excludes it from BOOT_COMPLETED, so init seeds full amber
# (Daylight UI scale: 255 = max) and the app takes over once launched.
#
# ── Diagnostic revision: two channels, one hwcomposer gate ────────────────
#
# Verified live on the DC-1:
#   • /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight-amber (chip i2c 2-003c) accepts writes up
#     to 255 and emits ZERO light. A physical no-op, not a permissions or
#     scaling problem.
#   • Android's own brightness pipeline (HWC setDisplayBrightness — the lights
#     HAL exposes no lights at all) DOES emit light, and that light is amber.
#     Which physical string it drives is still unknown; the other leds-class
#     node, /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight (chip i2c 5-003c), has never been
#     driven directly because DAC blocked it.
#
# Hypothesis for the dead amber node: the Daylight LED driver gates its output
# on a "hwcomposer is enabled" signal that the stock display stack supplied and
# a GSI does not. The chip device dirs expose the driver's custom knobs —
# can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold(_hw), display_on_backlight_on_
# delay_us, hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass, hwcomposer_disabled_
# backlight_off_delay_us, i2c_brightness, registers, screen_off_backlight_off_*
# — and `hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass` reads exactly like the
# escape hatch: bypass the "hwcomposer disabled ⇒ backlight off" rule. Set it
# on BOTH chips (harmless where output is already un-gated) so a brightness
# write can be attributed to a string rather than to the gate.
#
# Two things this makes possible without another flash:
#   • chmod on BOTH brightness nodes, so AmberControl can be pointed at either
#     one live (Settings.System key dc1_amber_node / dc1_white_mode — see
#     docs/amber.md);
#   • the chip attrs get written by init, because /sys/bus/i2c/devices/*-003c/*
#     is plain `sysfs` that init may write and the shell may not.

on post-fs-data
    # DAC gate for both leds-class channels: amber (2-003c) and the
    # never-yet-driven white/unknown channel (5-003c).
    chmod 0666 /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight-amber/brightness
    chmod 0666 /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight/brightness

    # Un-gate the driver's hwcomposer interlock on both chips (see above).
    write /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass 1
    write /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass 1

    # Out-of-box amber seed (Daylight UI scale: 255 = max), unchanged.
    write /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight-amber/brightness 255

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chmod 0666 /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight-amber/brightness
    chmod 0666 /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight/brightness

    # Re-assert: the display stack comes up between post-fs-data and here, and
    # if it (re-)initialises the chip the bypass bit may have been cleared.
    write /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass 1
    write /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass 1

    # ── Chip-state readback, the DAC way ─────────────────────────────────
    # Same goal as the debug-prop block below (see its comment for why chip
    # state is the instrument that makes the gate observable), but the
    # mechanism is a mode change rather than an init command: `read` is NOT an
    # Android init command — the command list is write/copy/copy_per_line/
    # chmod/setprop/... plus `readahead`, with no `read` — so those lines
    # cannot parse and init will log "invalid command 'read'" for each. Giving
    # the attrs a read bit gets the same data with `adb shell cat`, no props
    # and no trigger:
    #
    #   adb shell cat /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/registers        # chip 2 dump
    #   adb shell cat /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/registers        # chip 5 dump
    #   adb shell cat /sys/bus/i2c/devices/[25]-003c/i2c_brightness
    #   adb shell cat /sys/bus/i2c/devices/[25]-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass
    #
    # 0644, not 0666: read for everyone (driver state, nothing sensitive),
    # writes still root-only — and root ignores the missing write bit on the
    # attrs that ship 0200, so init's writes above keep working.
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/registers
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/registers
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/i2c_brightness
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/i2c_brightness
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold
    chmod 0644 /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold


# ── Chip-state readback: registers → debug props ──────────────────────────
# NOTE: `read` is not an init command (verified against AOSP init's documented
# command list); init will reject all eight lines below at parse time and the
# debug.dc1.* props will never be set. Kept as the record of intent — the
# chmod block in the boot_completed trigger above delivers the same readback
# through `adb shell cat`. Delete this trigger once that is confirmed on the
# device.
#
# The chip attrs are root-only (mode 0200/0600): the shell can neither read
# nor write them, but init can do both. `read` copies the first PROP_VALUE_MAX
# bytes of a file into a property, so this trigger turns `adb shell setprop
# debug.dc1.regs <n>` (any new value; debug.* is shell-writable) into a live
# view of both chips' driver state via `getprop`:
#
#   debug.dc1.regs.a / debug.dc1.regs.w   — `registers` dumps (2-003c / 5-003c)
#   debug.dc1.bri.a  / debug.dc1.bri.w    — `i2c_brightness` (driver's value)
#   debug.dc1.byp.a  / debug.dc1.byp.w    — hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass
#   debug.dc1.thr.a  / debug.dc1.thr.w    — can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold
#
# This makes the gate observable without eyes on the panel: if a brightness
# write changes i2c_brightness/registers on one chip but not the other, the
# gate (or the node mapping) is the difference. Reads have no driver side
# effects (plain sysfs show handlers); a failed read just leaves the prop set
# from the previous trigger fire.
on property:debug.dc1.regs=*
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/registers debug.dc1.regs.a
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/registers debug.dc1.regs.w
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/i2c_brightness debug.dc1.bri.a
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/i2c_brightness debug.dc1.bri.w
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass debug.dc1.byp.a
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass debug.dc1.byp.w
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c/can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold debug.dc1.thr.a
    read /sys/bus/i2c/devices/5-003c/can_not_see_backlight_brightness_threshold debug.dc1.thr.w